# November 20, 2026: Kings County, Prince Edward Island (Georgetown, Montague, Cardigan, Souris, St. Peters Bay and the
# Points East shore). Region pe drawn by tools/choose.py --claim with --state pe; the three earlier PEI batches (pe_bhrt,
# pe_rhhz, pe_tpau) hold Queens and Prince counties and nothing east of Orwell and Wood Islands. Sources opened in this
# session: the Canadian Register of Historic Places statement for each place (historicplaces.ca, read with
# tools/page.py --all; ids from the {{HPC row}} templates of the Wikipedia "List of historic places in Kings County,
# Prince Edward Island"); Tourism PEI's attraction pages (dates of operation; times only where the page gives them);
# the PEI Lighthouse Society's pages for Cape Bear and East Point; the Diocese of Charlottetown's Mass-times table
# (February 2026); the Diocese of Nova Scotia and PEI's Parish of Georgetown page; the Georgetown Historic Inn's own
# page; OpenStreetMap (tools/nom.py) for positions.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
def L(t,u): return [t,u]
def HP(t,i): return ['Canadian Register of Historic Places: '+t,'https://www.historicplaces.ca/en/rep-reg/place-lieu.aspx?id=%d'%i]
def TP(t,u): return ['Tourism PEI: '+t,u]
BATCH={'key':'pe_gdgy','label':'Kings County, Prince Edward Island','added':'2026-11-20 02:17'}
MASS=L('Diocese of Charlottetown: Mass times table, February 2026 (PDF)','https://dioceseofcharlottetown.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Masstimesfec2426.pdf')
PLACES=[
N('kings-county-courthouse-georgetown','Kings County Courthouse (Alexander W. Matheson Building)','Georgetown','pe','civic','standing',46.18406,-62.5332,'exact','view','drive','','1887',
  'The county courthouse of Kings County, built in 1887 to designs by William Critchlow Harris after fire in the old wooden courthouse destroyed valuable records; the townspeople asked for stone or brick. Lemuel A. Wilmot built it for $8,000, and its cornerstone was laid on July 21, 1887 for Queen Victoria\'s Golden Jubilee. It is the smallest of the three principal courthouses built on the Island for both the county and supreme courts, and the only one of its kind in stone. In 2002 it was also named for Alexander W. Matheson, premier from 1953 to 1959.',
  'Rock-faced Romanesque Revival walls that alternate red Island sandstone with grey Wallace sandstone from Nova Scotia, finely moulded grey arches over the ground-floor windows and doors, stone quoins and a clipped-gable roof, on a grey Wallace sandstone foundation (Register). Interior not checked; what the building is used for now was not checked.',
  'A courthouse striped in red and grey sandstone.',
  'Provincially designated building at 60 Kent Street on Kent Square, in the town grid laid out in 1768. Look from the street and the square; whether it opens to visitors was not checked.',
  [HP('Kings County Courthouse',7442)],brick='no'),
N('holy-trinity-anglican-georgetown','Holy Trinity Anglican Church','Georgetown','pe','worship','standing',46.18342,-62.53062,'site','view','drive','','1842',
  'An Anglican church finished in 1842 on the southeast corner of Kent Square, on land set aside for the Church of England when Charles Morris laid out Georgetown in 1768. It appeared on a sailing chart in 1839 while still being built. In 1860 the roof was lengthened by fifteen feet for a vaulted ceiling and the Perpendicular Gothic windows were put in. The church shrank with the town after shipbuilding declined, was restored from 1980, and in the summers ran brass-rubbing workshops; the Register says it no longer held regular services and that the Town of Georgetown bought it, and the Anglican diocese now lists it as deconsecrated.',
  'A wood-shingled Gothic Revival church with a three-stage square tower, a battlemented top with a crocketed finial at each corner, traceried pointed windows, a large traceried window behind the altar, false buttresses at the back corners and some of its stained glass (Register). The Register\'s photograph shows the tower and nave painted pale, with no brick in view. Interior not checked.',
  'A church that was a landmark on a sea chart before it was finished.',
  'Owned by the Town of Georgetown (Register); deconsecrated (Diocese of Nova Scotia and PEI, Parish of Georgetown page). 93 Grafton Street on Kent Square; look from the street. Whether it opens was not checked.',
  [HP('Holy Trinity Anglican Church',12046),L('Diocese of Nova Scotia and PEI: Parish of Georgetown','https://www.nspeidiocese.ca/prince-edward-island/parish-of-georgetown')],brick='no'),
N('st-davids-united-georgetown','St. David\'s United Church','Georgetown','pe','worship','standing',46.1821,-62.5341,'site','view','drive','','1837–1841',
  'A Church of Scotland congregation built this church on town lots granted in 1837, under its first minister Roderick MacAulay, some time between 1837 and 1841. George Monro Grant, later principal of Queen\'s University, served here in 1861–63. It joined the Presbyterian Church in Canada in 1875 and the United Church in 1925, and has since been renovated to look more as it first did.',
  'A wood-shingled Gothic Revival church whose square tower clips the front gable, with a flat roof, four corner finials joined by boards carved as battlements, scalloped shingles near the top, and tall traceried pointed windows whose top three panes are coloured glass (Register). Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Active congregation per the Register; 50 Grafton Street. Look from the street; service times not checked.',
  [HP('St. David\'s United Church',11965)],brick='?'),
N('georgetown-historic-inn','Georgetown Historic Inn (Wightman house)','Georgetown','pe','hotel','standing',46.1815,-62.533,'site','open','drive','','1840',
  'Built in 1840 as the house of the shipbuilder and merchant Joseph Wightman (1806–1887), who came from Dumfries in 1823, farmed and built ships at Wightman\'s Point and sat in the legislature for Kings County ridings as a Liberal. Over the years the house was a bank, a tearoom, a post office, a Masonic hall and a hardware store before it became an inn.',
  'A two-and-a-half-storey Classical Revival house with corner pilasters, eave returns, a front verandah, a decorated frieze on the gable ends and simple mouldings on some original windows (Register). Rooms and the dining room are open to guests (the inn\'s own page).',
  'A shipbuilder\'s house that has been a bank, a post office and a Masonic hall.',
  'A working inn at 62 Richmond Street, privately owned. Its own page: in season June 1 to September 30; dining room open to the public for breakfast and dinner June 15 to October 15, weekend hours in the off season.',
  [HP('Georgetown Historic Inn',1662),L('Georgetown Historic Inn','http://peigeorgetownhistoricinn.com/')],
  brick='?',hours='Dining room: breakfast and dinner daily, June 15 to October 15; weekends off season (inn\'s page)'),
N('roma-three-rivers','Roma at Three Rivers (Jean-Pierre Roma settlement)','Brudenell','pe','town','traces',46.18176,-62.55834,'site','open','walk','','1732–1745',
  'In 1732 the Frenchman Jean-Pierre Roma set up a fishing and trading settlement at the tip of Brudenell Point, with nine substantial buildings and gardens, strengthening the French hold on Île Saint-Jean. New Englanders destroyed it in 1745 after the siege of Louisbourg. The point lay empty until the early 1800s, when the Macdonalds leased it for a commercial and boat-building business. It was made a national historic site in 1933.',
  'Remains above and below ground of Roma\'s storage cellar, company house, blacksmith shop, workers\' house, ice house and trash pit, and of 19th-century buildings including the Macdonald establishment, over about a hectare (Register). The site today has reconstructed buildings, trails, a bread oven and a restaurant (Tourism PEI). Which remains can be seen on the surface was not checked.',
  'A French trading post burned by New Englanders in 1745.',
  'Run as a visitor site at 505 Roma Point Road, Brudenell (Tourism PEI); pets permitted. Tourism PEI gives dates of operation July 2 to September 6, open daily except Mondays; lunch 12 to 1:30 p.m. and sweets 2 to 4 p.m. Site opening times not checked.',
  [HP('Jean-Pierre Roma at Three Rivers National Historic Site',8459),TP('Roma at Three Rivers National Historic Site','https://www.tourismpei.com/attractions/roma-at-three-rivers-national-historic-site')],
  brick='?',hours='July 2 to September 6, daily except Monday (Tourism PEI; times not checked)'),
N('brudenell-island-pioneer-cemetery','Brudenell Island Pioneer Cemetery','Georgetown Royalty','pe','burial','traces',46.19651,-62.57914,'area','view','walk','','1804',
  'In 1803 thirty-nine Highland Scots led by James MacLaren of Balquhidder, Perthshire (MacLarens, MacFarlanes, Stewarts, Robertsons and Gordons) came by Pictou to the Brudenell River. Sailing past the little island, MacLaren\'s daughter Christina Gordon said she wished to be buried there; a year later she was its first burial. A small wooden church on the island, where MacLaren held Episcopal services in Gaelic until he died in 1818, is gone.',
  'A clearing in the middle of a wooded island of about two acres, with several sandstone grave markers and a large memorial cairn put up in 1903 (Register).',
  'An island graveyard chosen from the deck of a ship.',
  'The Register says the island lies in the Brudenell River near Brudenell River Provincial Park and is reached on foot over a causeway. Who owns the island was not checked; treat it as look-only until that is known.',
  [HP('Brudenell Island Pioneer Cemetery',11659)],th=[46.20148,-62.58103],thn='Mapped lot on Brudenell Island Boulevard (OpenStreetMap; whether it serves the causeway was not checked)',brick='no'),
N('knox-dam-victoria-cross','Knox\'s Dam (Montague hydro plant)','Victoria Cross','pe','water','ruins',46.1587,-62.6753,'site','view','walk','','1852–1955',
  'The Montague River was dammed for a grist mill as early as 1852. Donald MacKenzie built a dam here after 1901, and in 1907 the Montague Electric Company began making electricity for the town; the dam was raised in 1917 with 23-foot stone walls so the plant could supply Georgetown too. Maritime Electric took it over in 1932 and ran it until the turbine failed in 1955. William Knox, the last supervisor, bought it in 1956, and a fish ladder was added in 1977.',
  'Concrete dam walls across the Montague River, the remains of the power-plant building, and rushing water in thick woods, with signs, paths and picnic tables (Register). The supervisor\'s house nearby is a bed and breakfast (Register).',
  'The power plant that lit Montague until its turbine died in 1955.',
  'On Route 320 at Victoria Cross. The Register calls it a recreation and fishing area enjoyed by locals and visitors, with paths and picnic tables, but the present owner was not checked: look from the road or ask locally.',
  [HP('Knox\'s Dam',12038)],brick='no'),
N('garden-of-the-gulf-museum','Garden of the Gulf Museum (Montague Post Office and Customs House)','Montague','pe','civic','standing',46.16379,-62.647,'site','open','drive','','1887–1888',
  'Built in 1887–88 as the Montague Bridge post office and customs house to plans from Thomas Fuller\'s office in Ottawa, supervised by David Stirling and William Critchlow Harris, with bricks made locally by Robert Stewart, for $6,315.47. Of the three Fuller post offices on the Island it is one of two left and the most intact; only a large east chimney has gone. It is now the Garden of the Gulf Museum.',
  'Red brick Romanesque Revival walls with recessed round-arched windows and doorway, on high ground above the bridge and the wharves (Register; the Register\'s photograph shows the red brick with pale gabled dormers). The museum has exhibits on three floors (Tourism PEI). Original interior fittings not checked.',
  'A Dominion post office that won a village a public building.',
  'Community museum at 564 Main Street. Tourism PEI gives dates of operation June 16 to August 31, with guided tours; times not checked.',
  [HP('Garden of the Gulf Museum',9318),TP('Garden of The Gulf Museum','https://www.tourismpei.com/search/OperatorDetails/op_id/5087')],
  brick='yes',hours='June 16 to August 31 (Tourism PEI; times not checked)'),
N('montague-railway-station','Montague Railway Station (The Station)','Montague','pe','rail','standing',46.1646,-62.6482,'site','open','walk','','1905–1906',
  'The branch line from the main line to Montague Bridge was tendered in 1904 after years of agitation, and the station opened with a crowd and a train trip to Georgetown on Dominion Day, July 1, 1906. Prime Minister Wilfrid Laurier spoke here in 1910. The town bought the station in 1984 and made it a welcome centre; the rails were abandoned in 1989 and the bed is now part of the Confederation Trail.',
  'A hipped roof with wide flared eaves on large wooden brackets, a hipped dormer, wood shingles on walls and roof, panelled doors with transoms and several original nine-over-nine windows, above the marina (Register). Now a welcome centre with shops and a café (Register); interior not checked.',
  'Where the first train into Montague pulled in on Dominion Day 1906.',
  'Owned by the Town of Montague (Register), 1 Station Street by the marina and the Confederation Trail. Tourism PEI\'s page for the welcome centre refused this session, so hours not checked.',
  [HP('The Station',9374)],th=[46.1642,-62.64699],thn='Lot off Water Street (OpenStreetMap)',brick='?',hours='?'),
N('elmira-railway-station','Elmira Railway Station (Elmira Railway Museum)','Elmira','pe','rail','standing',46.43983,-62.06535,'site','open','drive','','1912',
  'Built in 1912 by F. S. MacDonald for $3,600 as the eastern end of the Prince Edward Island Railway; the branch from Harmony Junction opened on October 26, 1912. It had separate waiting rooms for men and women and an agent\'s office, with a freight shed and engine house beside it. Passenger trains on the Island ended in 1964 and the last freight ran in 1989; the station was restored for the province\'s centennial in 1973 and is a museum of the PEI Museum and Heritage Foundation.',
  'A wooden station with a hipped, flared-eave roof on brackets, a shed dormer, wood shingles, nine-over-nine windows and its name sign at the roof corner (Register); a section of track where trains were turned and a 1943 caboose (Register). The Register\'s photograph shows a red brick chimney. Inside, the station master\'s office, a model-train collection and a miniature railway (Tourism PEI).',
  'The end of the line.',
  'Provincial museum at 457 Elmira Road, Route 16A, on the Confederation Trail. Tourism PEI gives dates of operation June 1 to September 30 and sends readers to the museum\'s own site for hours, which refuses this tool; times not checked.',
  [HP('Elmira Railway Station',4604),TP('Elmira Railway Museum','https://www.tourismpei.com/attractions/elmira-railway-museum')],
  brick='some',hours='June 1 to September 30 (Tourism PEI; times not checked)'),
N('east-point-lighthouse','East Point Lighthouse and Fog Alarm Building','East Point','pe','light','standing',46.45252,-61.97198,'exact','open','drive','','1867',
  'Built in 1866–67 at the eastern tip of the Island, where the Gulf of St. Lawrence, the Northumberland Strait and the Atlantic meet. Its position was wrong on the Admiralty charts, which was blamed for the wreck of the British warship HMS Phoenix on a reef off the point in September 1882. In 1885 Bernard Creamer moved the tower with horses and greased timbers, and a fog alarm building was put up; in 1908 the eroding shore forced another move and the fog building was enlarged or rebuilt. The fog alarm went from steam to a compressed-air diaphone in 1917 and to electric in 1971.',
  'An octagonal tower in white shingles with a red iron lantern, a railed gallery, a moulded cornice and pedimented caps over the windows and door, above red sandstone cliffs (Register); beside it the shingled two-part fog alarm building of 1885/1908, which once held an engine room, coal room, boiler room and a basement cistern (Register). Guided and self-guided tours (Tourism PEI).',
  'The lighthouse blamed for a warship\'s wreck, dragged inland twice.',
  'Run by the Friends of Elmira Inc. (Register), 398 Lighthouse Road. Tourism PEI: open daily 10 a.m. to 6 p.m. from May to September, dates of operation May 18 to September 30. The PEI Lighthouse Society says mid-June through Labour Day.',
  [HP('East Point Lighthouse',19729),HP('East Point Fog Alarm Building',19205),TP('East Point Lighthouse','https://tourismpei.com/attractions/east-point-lighthouse'),L('PEI Lighthouse Society: East Point Lighthouse','https://peilighthousesociety.ca/index.php/lighthouses/open-lighthouses/46-east-point-lighthouse')],
  th=[46.45272,-61.97149],thn='Lot on Lighthouse Road (OpenStreetMap)',conf='mixed',brick='no',hours='Daily 10 a.m. to 6 p.m., May 18 to September 30 (Tourism PEI)'),
N('souris-historic-lighthouse','Souris Historic Lighthouse (Souris East Light)','Souris','pe','light','standing',46.34603,-62.24745,'exact','open','drive','','1880',
  'Designed by the Department of Marine and Fisheries and built by Peter Aylward in 1880, the 60-foot (18.5 m) tower on Knight Point once had a large attached keeper\'s dwelling, removed in 1959. When it was automated in 1961 it was one of four Canadian lights first given a 400-watt mercury vapour lamp, which became the standard. It is on the town\'s shield and flag and still guides boats into the port of the Magdalen Islands ferry.',
  'A tapered three-storey white-shingled tower with a round red iron lantern, a red-railed gallery on distinctive red brackets, pedimented window caps and a stone foundation (Register). Visitors climb to the lantern room and balcony; inside, an interpretive centre on the lighthouse, the port and sea glass (Tourism PEI).',
  'The light on the town flag.',
  'Interpretive centre at 134 Breakwater Street. Tourism PEI gives dates of operation June 15 to October 9; times not checked.',
  [HP('Souris Historic Lighthouse',18696),TP('Souris Historic Lighthouse','https://www.tourismpei.com/attractions/souris-historic-lighthouse')],
  brick='no',hours='June 15 to October 9 (Tourism PEI; times not checked)'),
N('st-marys-church-souris','St. Mary\'s Roman Catholic Church','Souris','pe','worship','standing',46.35976,-62.25231,'exact','open','drive','','1901–1902',
  'The third Catholic church in Souris, built in 1901–02 to designs by William Critchlow Harris with walls of sandstone quarried at Chepstow; the first, of 1838, burned in 1849 and its successor was outgrown. In 1929 a faulty flue set fire to the church and gutted it, leaving the sandstone walls, inside which John Marshall Hunter rebuilt it as a monument to Harris\'s work. The interior was much renovated in the 1950s.',
  'A large cruciform Gothic Revival church almost entirely of Island sandstone, with a west corner tower under a Neo-Baroque cap, a lower east tower with a conical cap, triple Gothic windows trimmed in freestone, buttresses and panelled wooden doors, on a rise above the Souris River (Register). The interior is the 1929–30 rebuilding and the 1950s renovation, not Harris\'s; not checked.',
  'Harris\'s sandstone walls survived the fire that took everything inside.',
  'Active parish church at 1 Longworth Street. The diocese\'s February 2026 table lists Mass at 9 a.m. on six days and Saturday confession at 11 a.m.; which days, and the weekend times, could not be read from the table\'s columns.',
  [HP('St. Mary\'s Roman Catholic Church, Souris',19171),MASS],
  conf='mixed',brick='no',hours='Mass 9 a.m. on six days of the week; confession Saturday 11 a.m. (diocese, Feb 2026; days not readable)'),
N('basin-head-cannery','The Cannery at Basin Head Fisheries Museum','Basin Head','pe','industry','standing',46.37822,-62.10945,'exact','open','drive','','1941',
  'The government finished this cannery in 1941, three years after a new harbour entrance was cut at Basin Head; the Eastern Fisheries Company packed "chicken haddie", canned pressed haddock and hake, here. It is the only original building on the site of the Basin Head Fisheries Museum, opened in 1973. A storm surge damaged it in January 2004 and it was repaired. The shore here became a Marine Protected Area for a rare Irish moss.',
  'A shingled wooden cannery standing out over the harbour on wooden pilings, with dormers, attached sheds and five wooden doors on the harbour side (Register). Inside, canning equipment and an ice boat that carried mail and passengers to the mainland in winter (Register).',
  'A 1941 fish cannery on stilts.',
  'Provincial museum at 336 Basin Head Road, Route 16, Kingsboro, beside the "Singing Sands" beach. Admission includes a tour of the Marine Protected Area (Tourism PEI). Dates of operation June 1 to September 28; times not checked (the museum\'s own site refuses this tool).',
  [HP('The Cannery at Basin Head Fisheries Museum',7436),TP('Basin Head Fisheries Museum','https://www.tourismpei.com/attractions/basin-head-fisheries-museum')],
  brick='no',hours='June 1 to September 28 (Tourism PEI; times not checked)'),
N('cape-bear-lighthouse','Cape Bear Lighthouse and Marconi Station','Beach Point','pe','light','standing',46.0036,-62.4579,'site','open','drive','','1881',
  'Built in 1881 where a light had stood since 1865. In 1906 the Department of Marine and Fisheries put a Marconi wireless station beside it, one of seven on the Island, to talk to the ice-breaking steamers Stanley and Minto; its operator, Thomas Bartlett, received the Titanic\'s distress call in April 1912, the first in Canada (Register; the PEI Lighthouse Society). The station closed in 1922. The keeper\'s dwelling was removed in 1960, the tower was moved back from the eroding shore in 1946 or 1947 (the sources differ), and a steel tower took over as the light in 2011.',
  'A tapered three-storey white-shingled tower with a red iron lantern and railed gallery, pediments over door and windows, cedar boards inside, and an addition that holds the Marconi museum and a gift shop (Register; the PEI Lighthouse Society).',
  'The station that heard the Titanic.',
  'Leased since 1998 to the Northumberland Community Development Corporation (PEI Lighthouse Society); 42 Black Brook Road, off Route 18, down a red clay road. The Society says it is open daily mid-June to mid-September, 10 a.m. to 6 p.m.; Tourism PEI gives July 1 to September 26.',
  [HP('Cape Bear Lighthouse',19728),L('PEI Lighthouse Society: Cape Bear Lighthouse','https://peilighthousesociety.ca/index.php/lighthouses/open-lighthouses/60-cape-bear-lighthouse'),TP('Cape Bear Lighthouse and Marconi Station','https://www.tourismpei.com/attractions/cape-bear-lighthouse-and-marconi-station')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no',hours='Daily 10 a.m. to 6 p.m., mid-June to mid-September (PEI Lighthouse Society); July 1 to September 26 (Tourism PEI)'),
N('panmure-head-lighthouse','Panmure Head Lighthouse','Panmure Island','pe','light','standing',46.14431,-62.46653,'exact','open','drive','','1853',
  'Finished in 1853 on land bought from Archibald MacDonald for 20 pounds, the first wooden lighthouse on the Island and the second of any kind after Point Prim (1845). Peter Stewart built it and Henry Williams finished it, on a site Captain Henry Bayfield chose to warn ships off Bear Reef and Panmure Ledge. A fog alarm building followed in 1908 and a keeper\'s house only in 1957; it was automated in 1985 and still works as a light.',
  'A rare octagonal four-storey tower in white shingles, with six-over-six windows under pediments and a round red iron lantern held to the bracketed gallery by iron rods, above red sandstone cliffs at the mouth of Cardigan Bay (Register). Wooden ladder stairs lead up to the lantern room; tours and a gift shop (Tourism PEI).',
  'The Island\'s oldest wooden lighthouse.',
  'Lighthouse and gift shop at 62–67 Lighthouse Road (the Register and Tourism PEI give different numbers), near Panmure Island Provincial Park. Tourism PEI gives dates of operation June 13 to October 13; times not checked.',
  [HP('Panmure Head Lighthouse',19723),TP('Panmure Island Lighthouse and Gift Shop','https://www.tourismpei.com/attractions/panmure-island-lighthouse-and-gift-shop')],
  brick='no',hours='June 13 to October 13 (Tourism PEI; times not checked)'),
N('st-peters-courthouse','St. Peter\'s Courthouse','St. Peters Bay','pe','civic','standing',46.41428,-62.58465,'site','view','drive','','1874–1875',
  'One of only six circuit courthouses the province built (1874–78) under the County Court Act passed the year the Island joined Confederation, and one of the first, to a standard provincial plan. It stood at the head of the bay facing the water and the railway, and heard mostly debt cases, at first every second month and later quarterly. It was also a school for grades 7 to 10 from 1936 to 1951, held its last court in 1968, and became a community hall after Albert Quigley bought it in 1954.',
  'A one-and-a-half-storey wooden courthouse with its gable to the road, wood shingles, narrow cornerboards, eave returns on the front gable, and the original round-headed windows from the old vestibule set into a modern porch (Register). Interior not checked.',
  'A country courthouse that became a school and then a stage.',
  'Owned by the Community of St. Peters Bay and used for plays and concerts (Register); 5697 St. Peters Road, Route 2, beside the Confederation Trail. Look from the road; performance times not checked.',
  [HP('St. Peter\'s Courthouse',20877)],brick='no'),
N('st-alexis-mission-rollo-bay','St. Alexis Mission (Acadian pioneer cemetery)','Rollo Bay East','pe','burial','traces',46.3517,-62.3178,'area','view','drive','','1804',
  'Acadian families who escaped the expulsion from Nova Scotia in 1755 hid first at Point Prim and then at Bay Fortune, slipping past Lord Rollo\'s deportation of 1758. When the proprietor resettled the Bay Fortune land in 1798 some moved on to Rollo Bay, where Father Angus Bernard MacEachern set up a small log church mission in 1804; Bishop Joseph-Octave Plessis of Quebec dedicated it to St. Alexis in 1812. The founding families named on the site are Bourke, Chiasson, Daigle, Longuepee and Pitre.',
  'Treed ground above Rollo Bay with an interpretive sign, an Acadian flag by the highway and two gravestones: Simon D\'Aigle, mariner, d. 1849, carved with a cross between two anchors, and Joseph McRae, a Scottish settler, d. 1858 (Register). Nothing is left of the log church.',
  'Two gravestones from the Acadians who hid from Lord Rollo.',
  'On Cemetery Road, Rollo Bay East. The Register describes a sign and flag by the highway but does not say who owns the ground; look from the road. Nothing was picked out on the aerial.',
  [HP('St. Alexis Mission',1884)],brick='no'),
N('all-saints-church-cardigan','All Saints Roman Catholic Church','Cardigan','pe','worship','standing',46.24295,-62.62182,'exact','open','drive','','1874',
  'Built between spring and November 1874 for the Irish and Scottish Catholics round Cardigan, on land from William Lewis and John Griffin, and blessed by Bishop McIntyre; the transept was added later. The last of several renovations was in 1995, when the sandstone foundation was replaced by a large basement. At the gate stands a stone Marian archway built for the Marian Year 1954 by the Cardigan mason James "Jimmy" Rice and others.',
  'A large shingled French Gothic church on a hill north of the village, with a segmented entrance tower whose spire rises between four small gables each topped by a cross, bracketed eaves, a transept with oculus windows, stick-style gables and Celtic crosses, and traceried pointed windows; inside, an Italian marble altar carved by a parishioner, William P. Lewis (Register). Interior not checked.',
  'A marble altar carved by one of the parish.',
  'Active parish church at 4279 Chapel Road, Route 321. The diocese\'s February 2026 table gives 9:30 a.m. and 10:30 a.m. for All Saints, but its columns could not be read to say on which days.',
  [HP('All Saints Roman Catholic Church',11344),MASS],brick='?',hours='?'),
N('st-pauls-church-sturgeon','St. Paul\'s Roman Catholic Church','Sturgeon','pe','worship','standing',46.1072,-62.5336,'area','open','drive','','1873–1888',
  'William Critchlow Harris\'s first Catholic church and his first in stone, begun in 1873 with sandstone from St. Mary\'s Road and finished in 1888; the interior was not done until it was dedicated in 1892. The parish priest, William Phelan of County Wexford, a trained stonemason like his brother James at Vernon River, did and directed much of the building himself, on his own land; the money came partly from the well-known Sturgeon Tea Parties. Phelan was buried in a vault under the church in 1921.',
  'Island sandstone trimmed with Nova Scotia freestone, a nave with side aisles and a round apse, a tall segmented side tower with a wooden spire, and a big three-lancet window under a rose window on the front; the aisle dormers were added later, and the first cross from the spire now stands in the cemetery (Register). A plaque to Father Phelan is by the main door. Interior not checked.',
  'A church built by its own priest\'s hands.',
  'Active parish church at 1133 Cambridge Road, Route 17A, beside its parochial house; the church was not found on the aerial at that address, so the pin marks only the area. The diocese\'s February 2026 table lists one 9 a.m. weekend Mass and no weekday Masses.',
  [HP('St. Paul\'s Roman Catholic Church',11275),MASS],brick='no',hours='One weekend Mass at 9 a.m., no weekday Mass (diocese, Feb 2026)'),
N('souris-town-hall','Souris Town Hall (former Post Office and Customs House)','Souris','pe','civic','standing',46.355,-62.2551,'site','open','drive','','1905',
  'Built in 1905 as the post office and customs house to plans from the federal Chief Architect\'s Branch; Bernard Creamer was the contractor and Edward J. Duffy the master mason, with sandstone from St. Catherine\'s. The post office was on the ground floor, customs upstairs and the caretakers on the third floor. The town bought it after the federal offices moved in 1960; it now holds the town offices and council chamber, provincial court sittings, and since the 1970s the Souris branch library.',
  'A three-storey Romanesque Revival building of Island sandstone with paler Wallace freestone trim, arched doorways, stone lintels and sills, a hipped roof with bracketed eaves and dormers, freestone steps and a date stone carved "1905" (Register). A plaque was unveiled by the Duke and Duchess of York in 1989. Interior renovated in 1988 (Register).',
  'A stone post office in a town of wooden buildings.',
  'Town of Souris, 75 Main Street. The library and town office inside are open to the public; their hours were not read (the province\'s library pages answer 403).',
  [HP('Souris Town Hall',18705)],brick='no',hours='?'),
N('matthew-mclean-building-souris','Matthew and McLean Heritage Building','Souris','pe','civic','standing',46.3547,-62.2534,'site','view','drive','','',
  'The store of Uriah Matthew (1842–1902) and John McLean (1846–1936), who trained at Heartz and Son in Charlottetown and opened a general business in Souris in 1869 with Benjamin Heartz\'s backing. The firm became the chief hardware and dry-goods merchant of eastern PEI, with a branch store and starch factory at Dundas (later moved to Bridgetown), lobster canneries in the 1880s, and trade to the Magdalen Islands. The building\'s own date was not given in the part of the statement read.',
  'A two-storey wooden store with large plate-glass storefront windows, round-arched upper windows under dramatic Gothic hood mouldings, a bracketed gable roof and a flat-roofed west wing with a bracketed parapet (Register). Present use not checked.',
  '',
  '95 Main Street, Souris; look from the street. The designation covers the exterior only.',
  [HP('Matthew and McLean Heritage Building',5983)],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('little-sands-church','Former Little Sands United Church','Little Sands','pe','worship','standing',45.9662,-62.6457,'site','view','drive','','1898',
  'Built in 1898 under D. J. MacLean for a Presbyterian congregation of Scottish settlers who had worshipped at Wood Islands; the opening service was preached in English and Gaelic. It joined the United Church in 1925 and was decommissioned when the pastoral charge built a new church at Murray River in 2009. The Friends of Little Sands Church took ownership in 2012.',
  'An odd mix found nowhere else on the Island: a Carpenter Gothic body with pointed traceried windows and a round "double Trinity" window, and a Neoclassical side tower with pedimented dormers and a tri-gabled verandah on columns, in vertical and horizontal clapboard on a stone foundation (Register). The Register says it has been vacant for many years and shows signs of decline. Interior not checked.',
  'A country church that cannot decide whether it is Gothic or Classical.',
  'Owned by the Friends of Little Sands Church (Register), 11352 Shore Road, Route 4, overlooking the Northumberland Strait. Look from the road.',
  [HP('former Little Sands United Church',5255)],brick='no'),
N('annandale-rear-range-light','Annandale Rear Range Lighthouse','Annandale','pe','light','standing',46.26634,-62.43682,'site','view','drive','','1901',
  'A 65-foot (19.8 m) tapered wooden range light built in 1901 to replace one of 1898 that a gale destroyed in 1900, guiding boats in from the Northumberland Strait to the Boughton River. When it was built potatoes and other goods still left the Island by water, and the light served shipbuilding, fish packing and the fishery. It is the tallest range light on the Island and sits on no fixed foundation so it can be moved.',
  'A tall, slender red-and-white shingled tower with its original square wooden lantern and four-over-four windows set off-centre low down and centred near the top, at the shore of Juniper Point (Register).',
  'The tallest range light on the Island.',
  'A federal heritage lighthouse on Lighthouse Road, Juniper Point, among houses (Register). Look from the road; who owns the ground round it was not checked.',
  [HP('Annandale Rear Range Lighthouse',22597)],brick='no'),
N('st-peters-harbour-lighthouse','St. Peters Harbour Lighthouse','St. Peters Harbour','pe','light','standing',46.44194,-62.74761,'site','view','walk','','c. 1878',
  'A square tapered wooden tower of about 1878, 10.4 m tall, on the west side of the entrance to St. Peters Bay, where the first French settlement on this shore began in 1719. The light here was one of sixteen on the Island before Confederation. It has been moved several times as the coast changed, became a warning light after the harbour was given up in 1951, and was decommissioned in 2008.',
  'A white shingled tower with a gallery and an unusual six-sided lantern, among high dunes with no building near, looking across to Prince Edward Island National Park (Register).',
  'A lighthouse that keeps being moved back from the sea.',
  'A federal heritage lighthouse at the end of Lighthouse Road, in a provincially protected area (Register). Who manages the ground and whether you may walk to the tower was not checked; look from the road\'s end.',
  [HP('St. Peters Harbour Lighthouse',21013)],th=[46.44125,-62.7478],thn='End of Lighthouse Road (seen on the aerial)',brick='no'),
]
